00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.460
If you ever wonder why the edges are always so blurry back in the day and so soft

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.320
It's because our hardware is not powerful enough to actually capture all that data

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.900
When they shot like the prequel movies and they're seeing that all in digital or they shot this

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:17.680
you couldn't capture all the color data as well as your luma data.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.680
So luma is like your black-and-white, how bright things are

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.820
Color is what color it is

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.740
Most likely they're shooting into a 4:1:1 compression and what the heck does that mean?

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:29.700
For every four pixels of Luma data you'd get, you'd only get one pixel of color data

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.120
so the problem that you would run into is if you're filming somebody on a green screen with a digital video camera

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.120
You would have big blocky chunks

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:40.409
even though it would look good when you just look at it with your eyeballs the actual color data

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.020
The actual green was 1/4 of the resolution of your image

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.400
So the edges will look chunky and you'd have to blur the heck out of it

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:51.920
Which is exactly what you see on the blurry edges of Taylor Lautner here. These days you don't do that anymore

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.500
with full-on 4:4:4 video spaces where for every single pixel of luma data you have a pixel of color data

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.060
and that's how far we've come these days

